update public.questions as q set explanation = v.e
from (values
('3a195e41-be98-4ca1-9184-1008db19450d'::uuid, $x$Arc length s = r θ with θ in radians: s = 8 × 0.75 = 6. In degrees the formula becomes s = π r θ / 180, so check the units of the angle before substituting.$x$),
('3a2a5d95-67bb-410d-b00b-75e0e5f58dd5'::uuid, $x$A view is a virtual table defined by a query. Its main security use is to restrict which rows or columns each user can see, without copying the data or storing it separately from the base table.$x$),
('3a33ab53-1f80-428c-b9ca-69fe4a51e94a'::uuid, $x$Stress = force ÷ cross-sectional area = 100 ÷ (2 × 10⁻⁶ m²) = 5 × 10⁷ Pa. Stress is force per unit area, so the area must be in m² to give pascals.$x$),
('3a8c74bf-f676-433e-8b30-4d0e11d0fbda'::uuid, $x$Horizontal component = F cos θ = 50 cos 30° ≈ 43.3 N. The sine gives the vertical component, which is 25 N, so that option swaps the two components.$x$),
('3c0d4502-d1d6-4dcd-bbc9-7bdb1efd4cf5'::uuid, $x$For y = sin(bx) the period is 2π/b. With b = 3 the period is 2π/3. The option π/3 is half this period, which is the usual slip.$x$),
('3c627471-2347-45e0-83b7-b178cafc14a1'::uuid, $x$For the mean of n independent observations, Var(x̄) = σ²/n. The standard error is σ/√n, the square root of this variance, so σ/n is wrong on both counts.$x$),
('3cd43538-219d-4641-a243-f684da4f8a12'::uuid, $x$Each sector angle is the category's share of the total multiplied by 360°: (30 ÷ 120) × 360° = 90°.$x$),
('3e7a533f-3fad-44e8-a17f-5eff371bb6b8'::uuid, $x$Integrate 6x² to get 2x³, then evaluate between the limits: [2x³] from 0 to 1 = 2(1)³ − 2(0)³ = 2. Forgetting the limits, or keeping 6x², does not give a number.$x$),
('3ebcd159-f629-433a-9fca-263f8674a5b1'::uuid, $x$The angles in a triangle sum to 180°, so C = 180° − 50° − 65° = 65°. To check a subtraction like this, add all three angles back to 180°.$x$),
('3ec565ba-7d44-4175-9bb9-22a62cfda31c'::uuid, $x$A geostationary satellite has a 24-hour orbital period, which needs a radius of about 42,000 km from the Earth's centre, roughly 36,000 km above the surface. It sits over one point on the equator.$x$),
('40c87fa5-6804-4c5a-bdfa-26742fe3d03e'::uuid, $x$y' = 3x² − 3 = 0 at x = −1, and y'' = 6x = −6 < 0 there, so the stationary point is a maximum. A stationary point with negative second derivative is a maximum.$x$),
('418cf98f-23bb-4756-8b30-630c425a4fdd'::uuid, $x$P(X ≥ 1) = 1 − P(X = 0) = 1 − e^(−0.5) ≈ 0.3935. The option 0.6065 is P(X = 0) itself, the complement of what is asked.$x$),
('41faac2d-5f84-46c9-a741-f8f49f247558'::uuid, $x$Scaling each value by b multiplies the variance by b², so 4² = 16. The standard deviation is multiplied by b, so it would scale by 4, not 16.$x$),
('4225783f-765d-4c98-aef6-7c133d8e6d0f'::uuid, $x$A Trojan horse looks like legitimate software but hides a malicious payload. Unlike a worm, it does not self-replicate; it relies on the user installing it.$x$),
('432eaa5b-9b1c-4515-bc0b-237c4886e6f0'::uuid, $x$The half adder's sum output is A XOR B, and its carry output is A AND B. The sum is 1 only when exactly one input is 1.$x$),
('4399364e-29e4-4379-b4a8-f3a717bd12c4'::uuid, $x$For a circular orbit v = √(GM/r), so v ∝ 1/√r. Halving r multiplies v by √2, so the speed increases by √2.$x$),
('43e48dde-2cfd-475a-8870-59dd0f5d2ac4'::uuid, $x$Redshift z = Δλ/λ, which equals v/c for speeds much less than c. It is a ratio with no units. Δλ × c has units of length times speed, and v²/c is not a redshift.$x$),
('44185c97-b897-43cd-a370-7b66601814d7'::uuid, $x$1 MB = 1024 KB, so 1024 KB = 1 MB. Each step from KB to MB to GB to TB multiplies by 1024.$x$),
('44218fbf-ad39-42f3-a15d-b4f8ce9335f3'::uuid, $x$This is De Morgan's law: the complement of a product is the sum of the complements, so (A·B)′ = A′ + B′. The option A′·B complements only one of the inputs.$x$),
('44682cb8-1455-4cfd-a252-72508db6cd35'::uuid, $x$Phishing is a form of social engineering: a deceptive message tricks the user into giving up credentials or other data. It attacks people rather than network hardware.$x$),
('44c6d39f-f44a-40ad-a01d-cf2d51b49e57'::uuid, $x$sin 30° = 1/2 exactly. √3/2 is sin 60°, and √2/2 is sin 45°, so those options are values for different angles.$x$),
('45d1357d-2b1f-4e55-aa90-605986c7261d'::uuid, $x$01100101 = 64 + 32 + 4 + 1 = 101. Bit values from the right are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64 and 128.$x$),
('46537a58-d619-4b1a-b0ff-a1434b998ad8'::uuid, $x$Midpoint = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2) = ((2 + 6)/2, (−3 + 5)/2) = (4, 1). Adding the coordinates without halving them gives (8, 2), which is wrong.$x$),
('46c4d9ff-7a3b-4a81-aa08-963e8f579aa2'::uuid, $x$In Young's double slit experiment the fringe spacing is x = λD/a, where D is the screen distance and a is the slit separation. Spacing grows with wavelength and screen distance, and shrinks as the slits are moved apart.$x$),
('47174e3d-f6e7-4fd5-80ac-ea182e6cee86'::uuid, $x$Mutually exclusive events cannot both occur, so P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) = 0.3 + 0.45 = 0.75. The product 0.135 applies to independent events, which is a different situation.$x$),
('475cf47e-e518-417b-8fb2-27b3d6bef853'::uuid, $x$Run-length encoding writes each run as its length followed by its symbol. Three As become 3A and three Bs become 3B, giving 3A3B. A3B3 reverses the order within each run.$x$),
('47b3b304-e8b1-4da3-9ddc-ea655e92b94a'::uuid, $x$E(X²) = Σ x² P(X = x) = 0 + 1 × 0.5 + 4 × 0.3 = 1.7. Square each value before weighting it by its probability; squaring E(X) gives a different number.$x$),
('481bc39d-4bfe-4ad2-9739-edd5cb5f8058'::uuid, $x$Volume = length × width × height = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60 cm³. The units are cubic centimetres, not centimetres.$x$),
('49053953-d557-400f-a8e0-ad94fa4e3095'::uuid, $x$dy/dx = 24x² − 6 = 0 gives x² = 1/4, so x = ±1/2. Stationary points come from setting the derivative to zero and solving for x.$x$),
('4a1c9b43-d868-4a87-b50a-a04b80b6d2f9'::uuid, $x$Adjacent nodes on a stationary wave are half a wavelength apart, λ/2. The distance from a node to the next antinode is λ/4, which is the option that is easy to confuse with it.$x$),
('4cef39e8-8ee5-4391-a920-ec6b5884bfae'::uuid, $x$Power = work ÷ time = 1000 J ÷ 20 s = 50 W. One watt is one joule per second.$x$),
('4d367a07-60ca-4e8b-81a7-b9ef8381e99b'::uuid, $x$Choosing 2 pens from 5 where order does not matter is ⁵C₂ = 5!/(2! 3!) = 10. Permutations are only needed when order matters.$x$),
('4d9b68bb-9f54-411a-a68a-e0eb725009a4'::uuid, $x$A game is fair when its expected winnings are zero, so neither side gains on average. E(winnings) = 0 is the definition of a fair game.$x$),
('4e4c0c08-2ba6-4b5f-bd50-60ca4ea48bb3'::uuid, $x$a · b = (1)(3) + (2)(−1) = 3 − 2 = 1. The dot product multiplies matching components and adds the results, giving a scalar rather than a vector.$x$),
('4eae7845-ae22-430e-a690-78ecfa726ffc'::uuid, $x$Conservation of momentum: 3 × 4 + 1 × 0 = (3 + 1)v, so 12 = 4v and v = 3 m s⁻¹. The option 12 m s⁻¹ is the total momentum, not the speed.$x$),
('4eb84692-f45f-4d29-ac7a-14bf4d1ccf95'::uuid, $x$The unbiased sample variance divides the sum of squared deviations by n − 1 (Bessel's correction), because estimating the mean from the sample uses up one degree of freedom.$x$),
('4f0b3010-27e9-4a12-86e2-173cdc112e24'::uuid, $x$Standard ASCII uses 7 bits per character, which gives 2⁷ = 128 characters. Each character is therefore not a fixed 8 or 16 bits.$x$),
('4f110c78-0503-4def-8d34-e44d40c20d94'::uuid, $x$sin 45° = √2/2 ≈ 0.707, which is the same value as cos 45°. ½ is sin 30° and √3/2 is sin 60°.$x$),
('4f80ad9e-d18d-43ad-b55d-01672de1cbcb'::uuid, $x$Variance is the square of the standard deviation, so s² = 3² = 9. The option 1.73 is √3, which is a square root rather than a square.$x$),
('500be090-0c57-4fd1-8ecb-089f84d69f21'::uuid, $x$Adding a constant to every value shifts the data but does not change how spread out it is. The standard deviation is unchanged; only the mean moves up by 10.$x$)
) as v(id, e)
where q.id = v.id;
